From 4a1365f16516c42c6e90153065b9a1d2e5fadc04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 13:19:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dom: implement Node.moveBefore
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

moveBefore repositions an already-attached node atomically. The difference
from insertBefore is observable: a remove-then-insert pair disconnects the
node, so an <iframe> reloads, focus is lost, animations restart, and a
render-blocking element stops blocking. A move never disconnects it.

Because the spec requires both parents to share a shadow-including root, a
moved node's connectedness cannot change — so the document's id index stays
valid and is deliberately not torn down and rebuilt, which is both the
correctness guarantee and the reason the operation is cheaper.

Observers still see the move: records are queued for the old and the new
parent, per spec. Only the disconnection is skipped, not the notification.

Pre-move validity is stricter than pre-insert in two ways that matter — the
node must already be in the tree (a move has nothing to preserve otherwise)
and must share the target's root — so callers relying on the atomic
guarantee get an exception rather than silently insert-shaped behaviour.

WPT issue #1491 problem 27,
dom/nodes/moveBefore/preserve-render-blocking-style.html: with no moveBefore
at all the test's script threw, the document was never styled, and it
rendered white against Chromium's 100% green.
